<h2>The case and its history</h2>
<p>FIR No. 55/2016 was registered by the Crime Branch and prosecuted under Sections 3 and 4 of MCOCA — the provisions punishing organised crime and the possession of property derived from it. By the time the present application under Section 483 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 was heard, the applicant&rsquo;s custody had stretched to approximately seven years and nine months. A co-ordinate consideration had refused him bail on 28 November 2025; the fresh application rested on the further passage of time and on the state of the trial record.</p>

<h2>The Court&rsquo;s weighing exercise</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The statutory bar.</strong> Section 21(4) MCOCA requires reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty and will not offend on bail — a threshold deliberately set high for organised-crime prosecutions.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The constitutional counterweight.</strong> The Court applied the line of authority holding that where trial delay makes the period of incarceration disproportionate, Article 21 permits bail notwithstanding statutory restrictions.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The trial arithmetic.</strong> Of 140 cited witnesses, 88 had been examined at the earlier stage — years into the trial, with a long road remaining.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The co-accused ledger.</strong> Several acquittals had already been recorded in the case, and co-accused had secured bail — both relevant to the applicant&rsquo;s comparative position.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court did not gloss over the applicant&rsquo;s record. His antecedents — including a conviction and a prior episode of absconding — were acknowledged expressly. But the function of antecedents at this stage, the order shows, is to calibrate conditions rather than to extend custody indefinitely once the length of detention and the pace of trial cross the constitutional line. Bail followed on a bond of INR 50,000 with two sureties, with conditions including fortnightly reporting to the investigating agency, surrender of passport, and a prohibition on contacting witnesses.</p>

<h2>Where this fits in the law on special statutes</h2>
<p>Courts have repeatedly reconciled the twin-condition structure of special statutes with the right to a speedy trial: the restriction on bail is valid, but it presupposes a trial that proceeds. When years pass, witnesses remain unexamined by the dozen, and the prosecution cannot forecast an end, continued detention stops being pre-trial custody and starts resembling punishment without adjudication. That is the point at which constitutional courts intervene — not to dilute the statute, but to prevent its machinery from doing what no statute may do.</p>
